A **component mounting positional deviation measurement unit** is a cassette-shaped calibration device that can be attached to the feeder setting section of a component mounting machine using the same guides, locating pins, lock mechanism, and—if available—electrical connector as a cassette-type feeder.

The unit typically includes:

- a rigid body having the external mounting geometry of a feeder;
- a measurement stage or transparent receiving plate located within the travel range of the mounting head;
- a reference mark defining a commanded mounting position;
- an imaging device, such as an upward-looking camera, together with illumination;
- a controller or interface for transferring measurement data to the mounting-machine controller; and
- optionally, a component collection or stage-cleaning mechanism for repeated measurements.

During calibration, the mounting head picks up a reference component, moves to a predetermined coordinate above the unit, and places or holds the component at the measurement position. The camera acquires an image of the component relative to the reference mark. Image processing determines the component center and orientation, and calculates deviations in the **X direction, Y direction, and rotational direction (θ)** from the commanded mounting position.

Because the unit is installed directly in a normal feeder slot, measurements are made under substantially the same mechanical and operating conditions as production mounting. The resulting deviation values can therefore be used to correct head offsets, nozzle offsets, camera-to-head coordinate errors, or other mounting-position parameters without placing a calibration board on the normal board conveyor.
